This manual describes the features that have been implemented in
Dale  as  well  as  documenting  future  changes  that should be
implemented.


DATA TYPES

Objects  in  Dale  are  tagged  with  a  type that is actually a
pointer  to  the  operations that are legal for that type.  Dale
supports  the creation of user data types by using the following
primitive  types.   { integers,arrays,object  pointers,booleans,
objects,code,block,anIndexableObject,strings and error }.

Objects are any Dale object.  It is the super class of the other
types.    code  is  an  object which can be executed.  It can be
thought  of  a a Dale statement.  block is an Dale block of code
represented  as  ($ some statements $).  anIndexableObject is an
object that has an array component that can be indexed.    Error
is a special internal object used for errors.


VALID OPERATIONS ON INTEGERS (3/9/84)

addition +  (anInteger + anInteger) -> anInteger

subtraction  -  (anInteger - anInteger) -> anInteger

multiplication *  (anInteger * anInteger) -> anINteger

division /  (anInteger / anInteger) -> anInteger

modulus !%  (anInteger !% anInteger) -> anInteger

equality =  (anInteger = anInteger) -> aBoolean

inequality !!=  (anInteger !!= anInteger) -> aBoolean

lessthan <  (anInteger < anInteger) -> aBoolean

lessthan or equal <=  (anInteger <= anInteger) -> aBoolean

greaterthan >  (anInteger > anInteger) -> aBoolean

greaterthan or equal >=  (anInteger >= anInteger) -> aBoolean

increment ++   (anInteger ++) -> anInteger

decrement --  (anInteger --) -> anInteger

printing print (anInteger print) -> anINteger

definateloops  TimesRepeat:  (anInteger  TimesRepeat:  block) ->
                              anObject
    (Note  that self  refers to the  integer  not to self of the
     outer environment {to be changed in the future.})


VALID OPERATIONS ON CODE (3/12/84)

printing print  (block print) -> code  
    (This  prints out  the lisp representation of the code.   In
     the  future  this  should be changed to  print out the Dale
     listing of source.)

evaluation eval  (block eval) -> anObject

looping WhileTrue: (aBlock WhileTrue: block) -> anObject
    (*** NOTE ***   The  block must  contain  a explicit  return
     statement that returns a boolean value.  Forgetting this is
     a source of many errors.)


VALID OPERATIONS FOR OBJECTS AND OBJECTPOINTERS (3/12/84)

creation new  (objectName new) -> anObjectPointer 

errors error  (anObject error astring)       aborts  program and
               displays the given message.

assignment   <-     (anObjectPointer   <-   anObjectPointer)  ->
                     anObjectPointer


VALID OPERATIONS ON INDEXEDOBJECTS (3/12/84)
  (also see operations on objects above)

creation new: (objectName new: anInteger) -> anIndexable

access basicAt: (anIndexableObject basicAt: anInteger) -> 
                anObject

access basicput: at: (anIndexableObject basicput: anObject
                     at: anInteger) -> anObject


VALID OPERATIONS ON BOOLEANS (3/9/84)

creation new: (boolean new: anInteger) -> aBoolean

conditionals ifTrue:  (aBoolean ifTrue: block) -> anObject

conditionals ifFalse:  (aBoolean ifTrue: block) -> anObject

printing print (aBoolean print) -> aBoolean


VALID OPERATIONS ON ARRAYS (3/9/84)

creation new:  (array new: anInteger) -> anArray

retrieval at:  (anArray at: anInteger) -> anObject

insertion  put:  at:    (anArray put: anObject at: anInteger) ->
                        anObject


VALID OPERATIONS ON STRINGS (3/9/84)

creation !" !"  (string !" ascii characters !") -> aString 
   (Note that their must be a space after the first " and before
    the second " since the " can be part of a Dale message name)

printing print  (aString print) -> aString
   (prints with out line feed.)

printing lprint (aString print) ->aString 
   (prints with a line feed.)


VALID MESSAGES TO OPTION (3/10/84)

question ?  (option ?) -> 1
   This prints a list of all system options at the present time.


SPECIAL VARIABLES

  Self is a special variable that refers to the object receiving
the  message.    By  using  self  that object  is searched for a
matching  primary  message  then  its  super  classes.  A second
special  variable should be super which is just like self except
that the  superclasses of self  are searched  and not the object
itself.   This in not  implemented  as yet but  should be in the 
future.  By implementing super one can define a primary  message
name  that is  the same  as  the  superclass's and still use the
super classes method since the  new definition will not be found
in the  search.   This can be  gotten around  by using an unique
message name.


ENVIRONMENTS

  The  internal  environment  is  as  yet  unsupported.  A third
environment   should  also  be  created  called  Control.   This
environment  is  just  like the external environment except that
self   and  the  environment  contexts  will  not  refer  to the
receiving  object  but  instead  will refer to the outer object.
This  allows control structures to behave like we expect control
structures to behave, by not introducing a new environment.


TYPES

  Type  checking  and  generation  is  as yet unsupported.  Type
declarations are ignored and a objectpointer type is assumed. In
the  future type checking should be supported especially if dale
is  compiled  since type checking at compile time can catch some
user  errors  and  type  information can be used to produce more
efficient code.


USER OPTIONS

  The user can change  how top  level evaluation  prints out the
returning results.    Eithor  the  vector  representation of the
object  is  printed  or only  integers  and  boolean  values are
printed.
  
   Option  object-print-off;  % Prints integers & boolean values
   Option Object-Print-On; %Prints out the vector representation
                           %This is good when debuging packages.


USING DALE  (as of January 20 1984.)

  Dale   is   implemented  in  PSL  and  must  be  used  in that
environment after being loaded.

  To use  Dale on  the UG machine  (Unix), at the > prompt enter
the nmode editor.

1> nmode

You  will  now  be  in nmode which is an editor much like emacs.
Then type in (dskin "dale-primitives.sl") {lisp-e} this compiles
and loads in dale.   Lisp-e  is like the meta  command in emacs.
It executes the lisp  expression at  the Cursor.  (see the NMODE
manual for more details)

You  are  now  ready to try dale out.  To do so you must enclose
all dale  statements in (i) <statements> #.  Then to execute it,
place the CURSOR at the (i) and type {lisp-e}.   For example try
these:

(i) 5 + 6 print;# {lisp-e}
11
[3 INTEGER 11]
NIL
% notice that the !" must have a space before and after it.
(i) 3 TimesRepeat: ($ string !" hello !" lprint;$);# {lisp-e}
HELLO
HELLO
HELLO
[3 INTEGER 3]
NIL
(i)
class test
  superclass: object
  instance methods:
    tryit aNumber
      $(
         string !" your number is_ !" print;
         return aNumber print;
       $)
endclass.# {put cursor at (i) then  lisp-e}
NIL
(i) test new tryit 5;# {lisp-e}
YOUR NUMBER IS_5
[3 INTEGER 5]
NIL
(i)
$(
    var a;b.
  a <- 3;
  b <- 5;
  a < b print;
  a print;
  return b;
$) eval;#  {put cursor at (i) then  lisp-e}
TRUE
3
[3 OBJECTPOINTER [3 INTEGER 5]]
NIL
(i)
function foo
  method:
    self-msg$ anObject
    ($ anObject print; return 1; $)
end. {put cursor at (i) then Lisp-e}
NIL
(i) 3 + foo(7);# {lisp-e}
7
[3 INTEGER 4]
NIL

